// Video 1 — explore the Telemetry table after local-file ingestion.
// Run these in the default queryset (IoTEventhouse_queryset) attached to the IoTEventhouse KQL database.
// These are the exact queries run in the video. Run one at a time.

// 1. Confirm every sample row arrived. Result in the video: 6,000.
Telemetry
| count

// 2. Preview a handful of rows and check that the columns look right.
// take returns whichever rows it finds first, not a random sample of the whole table.
Telemetry
| take 10

// 3. Average temperature and number of readings per site. Result in the video: 4 records.
Telemetry
| summarize AvgTemp = avg(Temperature), Readings = count() by Site
| order by AvgTemp desc

// 4. The spiked readings. Status is ALERT when Temperature is above 32. Result in the video: 69 records.
Telemetry
| where Status == "ALERT"
| project Timestamp, DeviceId, Site, Temperature
| order by Timestamp asc

// 5. Inspect the stored schema, including column types.
// This is a management command, not a tabular query.
.show table Telemetry schema as json
